The attached image (Figure 2 from the PDF) illustrates the in vivo kinetics and biodistribution of Nano-11 nanoparticles after intramuscular injection. Panel A shows real-time fluorescence imaging in mice, demonstrating that Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 remains at the injection site over 21 days, with fluorescence gradually decreasing over time. Panel B, showing excised organs on day 21, confirms Nano-11's localization primarily to the injection site with no significant accumulation in internal organs. Panel C quantifies the injection site fluorescence, indicating a significant decrease in intensity from day 2 to day 21, suggesting gradual clearance.

The image significantly contributes to applications in Vaccine development by providing critical evidence regarding the safety and efficacy profile of Nano-11 as an adjuvant. Its localized retention at the injection site and gradual clearance without systemic distribution are highly desirable characteristics for vaccine adjuvants, minimizing potential off-target toxicities. This data supports the rational design of new vaccines, ensuring the adjuvant effectively enhances immune responses while maintaining a favorable safety profile, which is paramount for prophylactic vaccine candidates.